            <ArticleTitle>Sacral kyphoplasty for the treatment of painful sacral insufficiency fractures and metastases.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>113-20</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.spinee.2012.01.019</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND CONTEXT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Sacral insufficiency fractures and metastases are a source of severe intractable pain, with limited therapeutic options. Sacroplasty has demonstrable efficacy and safety; sacral kyphoplasty, however, is rarely reported.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the safety and efficacy of sacral kyphoplasty for sacral insufficiency fractures and metastases.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective, with long-term follow-up; rural community-based practice.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT SAMPLE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Patients with sacral insufficiency fractures and metastases.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Numerical pain rating scale, opioid equivalent usage. SELF-REPORT MEASURE: Numerical pain rating scale. FUNCTIONAL MEASURE: Opioid equivalent consumption.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective analysis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Statistically significant improvement in pain; overall, an improvement in opioid consumption.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Sacral kyphoplasty appears to be a safe and efficacious procedure, comparable to sacroplasty, in the treatment of SIFs and sacral metastases.</AbstractText>
